{
 "segments": [
  {
   "text": "Case Thirteen.",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 1.923,
   "pause": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "The Misspelled Note.",
   "start": 2.5229999999999997,
   "end": 3.718,
   "pause": 1.4
  },
  {
   "text": "Down by the slipway there is a hand painted sign that says No Parking on the Pier. At least, that is what it means to say. Hedley Truscott painted it himself last spring, and he spelled pier p, e, i, r. Agnes Bell has pointed this out to him at least four times.",
   "start": 5.118,
   "end": 22.419,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "\"I before E, Hedley,\" she reminds him. \"P, i, e, r.\"",
   "start": 23.269,
   "end": 27.216,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "\"Looks right to me,\" says Hedley, every time.",
   "start": 28.066,
   "end": 30.754,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Captain Quill's most treasured possession is a ship in a bottle, a tiny three masted sailing ship with sails no bigger than a fingernail. It sits on a shelf in his harbour office. On Friday afternoon, the shelf was empty, and in the ship's place was a folded note.",
   "start": 31.604,
   "end": 48.5,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The note said: Don't worry. Your ship will be back by the peir at sunset. And the word pier was spelled p, e, i, r.",
   "start": 49.35,
   "end": 56.774,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Captain Quill brought the note straight to the Kettle and Gull. Agnes read it twice.",
   "start": 57.624,
   "end": 62.275,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Three people had been in and out of the harbour office that afternoon. Tamsin Trevelyan had dropped off a book about knots. Jago Penhallow had delivered a pasty. And Hedley had been fixing a loose board in the office floor.",
   "start": 63.125,
   "end": 76.885,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Tamsin had just finished writing a poster for her shop window. It said Story Time on the Pier, Saturday, and the word pier was spelled perfectly. Jago's chalkboard outside the bakery said Pasties, Two Doors from the Pier, and Jago had spelled it perfectly too.",
   "start": 77.735,
   "end": 94.503,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Can you solve it? Who left the note and borrowed the ship in a bottle?",
   "start": 95.603,
   "end": 99.165,
   "pause": 1.2
  },
  {
   "text": "Think about it.",
   "start": 100.365,
   "end": 101.389,
   "pause": 0.9
  },
  {
   "text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
   "start": 102.289,
   "end": 107.516,
   "pause": 4.5
  },
  {
   "text": "The Solution.",
   "start": 112.016,
   "end": 113.04,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Hedley Truscott borrowed the ship in a bottle.",
   "start": 114.14,
   "end": 116.615,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The note spelled pier as p, e, i, r. That is exactly the mistake on Hedley's sign by the slipway, the one Agnes has corrected again and again, and which Hedley still thinks looks right. Tamsin's poster and Jago's chalkboard both spelled pier correctly that very same day.",
   "start": 117.465,
   "end": 136.089,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "When Agnes showed Hedley the note, he grinned sheepishly. Captain Quill's birthday was on Sunday, and Hedley had been building him a second, bigger ship in a bottle as a present. He needed the original to copy the rigging. He had left the note so the captain wouldn't worry.",
   "start": 136.939,
   "end": 153.387,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "At sunset, the ship was back on the pier, just as promised. On Sunday, the captain received a second one, slightly lopsided and absolutely beautiful. The card that came with it, Agnes noticed, said Happy Birthday from Hedley, Down by the Peir.",
   "start": 154.237,
   "end": 169.938,
   "pause": 0.0
  }
 ],
